---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { RED_BELOW, GREEN_FROM } from '../lib.js';
---
<Base title="About" description="How my family is eating around the world from A–Z, one breakfast at a time.">
  <section class="page-head">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      We’re touring the world through breakfast recipes in nearly alphabetical order. Once or twice a week, we make breakfast from a different country, inventing a recipe that we think will be a great breakfast while also being true to the country of origin. These are my own versions, developed from a mix of recipes and adjusted for ingredients I can get locally. I aim for about two hours of hands-on work, but sometimes a recipe is worth breaking that guideline.
    </p>
    <p class="lede">
      There are 195 countries in the world, which means this project will take a while! Everyone at my house eats the breakfast and gets to rate it. I average the adults’ ratings and the kids’ ratings separately, then roughly weight the adults at 60% and the kids at 40%. Very often the kids will hate a dish while the adults love it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="intro" aria-labelledby="about-heading">
    <div class="intro-text">
      <h2 id="about-heading">About the project</h2>
      <p>My family is eating our way around the world, one breakfast at a time. I pick a country, choose a dish to explore, and cook our version. Most choices are breakfast dishes; when we take a different route, the post explains why. Then everyone rates it on a scale of 1–5.</p>
      <p>Some recipes turn into new favorites. Some we’ll never make again (sorry, saltfish). Every post has the full recipe, so you can try them at home.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="goals">
      <h2>Goals</h2>
      <ol class="goal-list">
        <li>
          <h3>Delicious</h3>
          <p>The food has to taste good. I tweak recipes until I think they’ll be the best possible recipe I can create. Although authenticity is important, I want my kids and wife to eat it.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Local ingredients</h3>
          <p>Everything comes from stores near me. If I can’t find something, I swap it. You’ll see the recipes reflect what I have on hand instead of ingredients specific to that region.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>About two hours of cooking</h3>
          <p>About two hours of hands-on work is the goal, not a hard limit. Some recipes need preparation the night before or longer rests. Each post includes estimated hands-on, cooking, and total time.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="rating-guide" aria-labelledby="rating-heading">
    <h2 id="rating-heading">How we rate</h2>
    <p>Everyone rates each breakfast out of 5. I average the adults’ ratings and the kids’ ratings separately, then combine those averages with roughly 60% of the weight on the adults and 40% on the kids. This is a family project, not a scientific study, and I haven’t always been perfectly consistent. The scores here are the ones I recorded at the time.</p>
    <p>We’re rating our experience of a particular recipe, including our substitutions and cooking results. A low score describes our preferences, rather than the country’s cuisine.</p>
    <ul class="legend rating-meanings" aria-label="What the scores mean">
      <li><span class="swatch tone-high"></span>{GREEN_FROM.toFixed(1)}–5.0: Plan to make it when we need a good breakfast idea</li>
      <li><span class="swatch tone-mid"></span>{RED_BELOW.toFixed(1)}–{(GREEN_FROM - 0.1).toFixed(1)}: Would eat again, but unlikely to enter the breakfast rotation</li>
      <li><span class="swatch tone-low"></span>Under {RED_BELOW.toFixed(1)}: Wouldn’t eat it again</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <p class="sign-off">Hopefully you enjoy making these recipes as much as we did!</p>
</Base>
